/**
@page quickstart Quickstart

@brief Integrate the library into an Arduino PLC IDE project.

This walks through delivering Botanik into an Axel PLC IDE project: producing the
library zip, importing it, and writing the thin entry point that runs your site. It
assumes you already have a site building under the simulator (see @ref setup).

@section qs_package Package the library

The library ships as an Arduino zip. The packaging step just copies and zips text,
so it runs from any configured tree - no OPTA toolchain needed to produce the
deliverable:

@code{.sh}
make -C build arduino-lib      # -> build/arduino/libbotanik.zip
@endcode

The zip holds `libbotanik` only (the umbrella headers plus the `libbotanik/` tree);
it carries no entry point of its own. The IDE compiles it with the OPTA board's own
flags.

@section qs_import Import it

In the IDE, use *Sketch -> Include Library -> Add .ZIP Library* and point it at
`build/arduino/libbotanik.zip`.

@section qs_pieces How the pieces map to the IDE

The Axel PLC IDE owns the sketch and only accepts imported Arduino libraries - you
cannot add a sketch folder or an arbitrary file tree. So the project is split three
ways:

| IDE side | Reference here | Role |
|----------|----------------|------|
| The "sketch" window | `botanik_fw.ino` | Thin: `setup()` calls `botanik_setup()`, `loop()` calls `botanik_step()`. |
| A C++ file at the project root | `botanik_arduino.cpp` + your site + `firmware.{h,cpp}` | Includes `<libbotanik.h>`, constructs the firmware, and *defines* `botanik_setup()` / `botanik_step()`. |
| Imported library | `libbotanik` (the zip) | The building blocks. |

`botanik_setup()` / `botanik_step()` are **not** library API - they are declared in
`botanik_arduino.h`, defined by your project-root file, and called by the sketch.
This indirection exists because the imported library cannot ship its own `.ino`.

@section qs_sketch The sketch

The sketch window is deliberately trivial - it mirrors what the IDE's generated
program calls into:

@code{.cpp}
#include "botanik_arduino.h"

void setup() {
    botanik_setup();
}

void loop() {
    botanik_step();
}
@endcode

The project-root C++ file does the real work: it includes `<libbotanik.h>`,
statically allocates your site's firmware (a `GreenhouseMLB41` or equivalent),
binds the PLC shared memory from `HAL::get_plc_input()` / `HAL::get_plc_output()`
rather than allocating it locally, and defines the two hooks - `botanik_setup()`
doing the construction/`setup()` work and `botanik_step()` running one main-loop
iteration. See `src/botanik_fw/` for the reference wiring.

@note The exact hook names (`botanik_setup` / `botanik_step`) are placeholders
pending confirmation of the symbol the Axel runtime calls into; adjust them to match.

@section qs_abi Checking the ARM build without the IDE

For a fast "does it still build and fit for the target" check without the full
Arduino link, compile the library (plus the reference wiring) for ARM directly:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B build-opta -DBOTANIK_TARGET=OPTA
make -C build-opta
@endcode

This exercises size, LTO, and the footprint `static_assert`s. Use `arduino-lib`
plus the IDE to produce the actual flashable image.
*/
